---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "nameservers",
 "name": "GoDaddy",
 "title": "How to Change Nameservers in GoDaddy",
 "description": "Change nameservers in GoDaddy step by step: where the setting lives, what values to enter, and the propagation window to expect.",
 "intro": "Nameserver changes are delegation changes: you're telling the internet which servers hold your domain's records. Here is exactly where GoDaddy keeps that setting and what to expect.",
 "panel": "Domain Settings page (not the DNS records page): Nameservers section, Change Nameservers. Choose GoDaddy's default (use GoDaddy nameservers) or Custom to point at another host such as Cloudflare.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Open Domain Settings",
   "My Products, the domain, Domain Settings tab (not DNS)."
  ],
  [
   "Nameservers, Change",
   "Scroll to Nameservers, click Change Nameservers, pick Enter my own nameservers (custom)."
  ],
  [
   "Paste and save",
   "Enter the two nameservers your new DNS host gave you, remove leftovers, Save. Registry delegation updates within the hour, full propagation up to 48 hours."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "GoDaddy aggressively offers DNS add-ons and parking during the flow; also, after a nameserver change to an external host, GoDaddy's own DNS Records page stops being authoritative even though it still shows your records.",
 "faqs": [
  [
   "Is GoDaddy DNS free?",
   "Yes, DNS hosting for domains registered there is included; the paid items are add-ons (premium DNS, SSL), not basic records."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in GoDaddy?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find GoDaddy's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for GoDaddy to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in GoDaddy?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in GoDaddy?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in GoDaddy, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
